{
  "name": "banking-parity",
  "note": "Every refusal path a ledger has. A runtime that ACCEPTS what the other refuses is the failure worth catching, and in banking that failure is money appearing from nowhere. The debit gates are walked one at a time so a runtime skipping a single given is caught by the step only that given refuses. The last step passes a non-numeric amount, so the comparison itself must fail — proving the two runtimes word an EVALUATION failure identically, not just a rule failure.",
